## CONVERSATION

<USER_REQUEST>
A particle is projected upwards from the ground with an initial velocity of \( 20 \, \text{m/s} \). Assuming the acceleration due to gravity is \( 9.8 \, \text{m/s}^2 \) downward and neglecting air resistance, find the maximum height reached by the particle.
</USER_REQUEST>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
The maximum height is achieved when the velocity of the particle becomes zero. We can use the kinematic equation:
\[
v^2 = u^2 + 2as
\]
where:
- \( v \) is the final velocity
- \( u \) is the initial velocity
- \( a \) is the acceleration
- \( s \) is the displacement (maximum height in this case)

Given:
- \( u = 20 \, \text{m/s} \)
- \( v = 0 \, \text{m/s} \) (at maximum height)
- \( a = -9.8 \, \text{m/s}^2 \) (acceleration due to gravity)

Substituting these values into the equation:
\[
0 = 20^2 + 2(-9.8)s
\]
\[
0 = 400 - 19.6s
\]
Solving for \( s \):
\[
19.6s = 400
\]
\[
s = \frac{400}{19.6}
\]
\[
s \approx 20.408 \, \text{m}
\]

Thus, the maximum height reached by the particle is \(\boxed{20.41 \, \text{m}}\).
</ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>

