","The coefficient of friction is defined as the ratio of the force required to overcome the friction to the normal force. The normal force is the force exerted by a surface on an object in parallel to the surface.  

\[
\mu = \frac{F_f}{F_n}
\]  
For example, the force of friction is \( F_f = \mu N \), where \( \mu \) is the coefficient of kinetic friction and \( N \) is the normal force.  

The normal force is the force per area, so \( F_n = p A \), where \( p \) is the pressure and \( A \) is the area. The pressure is \( p = \frac{F}{A} \), where \( F \) is the force and \( A \) is the area.

The standard units are:  
Force \( F \): \([MLT^{-2}]\) (from \( F = ma \))  
Area \( A \): \([L^2] \).  
\[
\boxed{\text{Coefficient of friction}} = \frac{\text{Force}}{\text{Force}} = \frac{[M L T^{-2}]}{[M L T^{-2}]} = [M^0 L^0 T^0]
\]  
Thus, the coefficient of friction is a dimensionless quantity.  

The correct answer is \(\boxed{c}\).",ground_truth_similarity,"# Task Description
