\[
\int_{-1}^{2} \left[(x^{2} + 3) - 1\right] \, dx = \int_{-1}^{2} \left(x^2 + 2\right) \, dx.
\]
The antiderivative of \(x^2 + 2\) is \(\frac{1}{3}x^3 + 2x\). Thus, the definite integral is:
\[
\left[\frac{1}{3}x^3 + 2x\right]_{ -1}^{2} = \left(\frac{1}{3}(2)^3 + 2(2)\right) - \left(\frac{1}{3}(-1)^3 + 2(-1)\right).
\]
