\[
B = \frac{\mu_0 N I}{2r}
\]

where:
- \( B \) is the magnetic field strength (in Tesla)
- \( \mu_0 \) is the permeability of air (4π × 10⁻⁷ T·m/A)
- \( N \) is the number of turns in the coil
- \( I \) is the current (in Amperes)
- \( r \) is the radius of the coil (in meters)

Substituting the given values:
- \( N = 100 \)
- \( I = 1 \, \text{A} \)
- \( r = 0.2 \, \text{m} \)
- \( \mu_0 = 4\pi \times 10^{-7} \, \text{T·m/A} \)

\[
B = \frac{(4\pi \times 10^{-7} \, \text{T·m/A}) \times 100 \times 1 \, \text{A}}{2 \times 0.2 \, \text{m}}
\]

\[
B = \frac{4\pi \times 10^{-5} \, \text{T·m}}{0.4 \, \text{m}}
\]

\[
B = 10\pi \times 10^{-5} \, \text{T}
\]

\[
B = 10\pi \times 10^{-5} \, \text{T} \approx 3.14 \times 10^{-4} \, \text{T}
\]

Thus, the magnetic field strength at the center of the coil is \(\boxed{3.14 \times 10^{-4} \, \text{T}}\).",relevance,"# Task Description
