Use momentum conservation to calculate the approximate recoil velocity of the Earth when a cannonball is fired. The cannonball has a velocity of 90.0 m/s and a mass of approximately 1 kg. The Earth's mass is \(5.97 \times 10^{24}\) kg.
</USER_REQUEST>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
To solve this problem, we can use the conservation of momentum. The total momentum of the system (bullet + Earth) must be conserved. The system is initially at rest, so the initial momentum is zero. After the bullet is fired, the momentum of the bullet is equal and opposite to the momentum of the Earth.

The conservation of momentum equation is:

\[
m_b v_b = - m_e v_e
\]

where:
- \(m_b\) is the mass of the bullet = 1.0 kg
- \(v_b\) is the velocity of the bullet = 90.0 m/s
- \(m_e\) is the mass of the Earth = \(5.97 \times 10^{24}\) kg
- \(v_e\) is the velocity of the Earth (the recoil velocity)

Rearranging to solve for \(v_e\):

\[
v_e = -\frac{m_b v_b}{m_e}
\]

Substituting the given values:

\[
v_e = -\frac{(1.0 \text{ kg})(90.0 \text{ m/s})}{5.97 \times 10^{24} \text{ kg}}
\]

\[
v_e = -\frac{90.0}{5.97 \times 10^{24}} \text{ m/s}
\]

\[
v_e \approx -1.51 \times 10^{-23} \text{ m/s}
\]

The negative sign indicates that the Earth moves in the direction opposite to the bullet. The magnitude of the velocity is \(1.51 \times 10^{-23}\) m/s.

Thus, the approximate recoil velocity of the Earth is \(\boxed{1.51 \times 10^{-23} \text{ m/s}}\).
</ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>

