","The maximum frequency shift in the Compton scattering is given by:  
\[
\Delta \lambda_{\text{max}} = \lambda_{\text{scatter}} - \lambda_{\text{incident}} = \lambda_0 (1 - \cos \theta)
\]  
where:  
- \( \lambda_{\text{scatter}} \) is the wavelength of the scattered photon,  
- \( \lambda_{\text{incident}} \) is the wavelength of the incident photon,  
- \( \lambda_0 = \frac{hc}{E} = \frac{hc}{\hbar \omega} \) is the Compton wavelength, and  
- \( \theta \) is the scattering angle.  
The maximum frequency shift occurs when \( \theta = 0^\circ \), so \( \cos \theta = 1 \). Thus, the maximum frequency shift is:  
\[
\Delta \lambda_{\text{max}} = \lambda_0 (1 - 1) = 0
\]  
This implies that the maximum change in wavelength is 0.  

The maximum change in wavelength occurs when the scattering angle is \( \theta = 180^\circ \), so \( \cos \theta = -1 \). The change in wavelength is:  
\[
\Delta \lambda = \lambda_{\text{scatter}} - \lambda_{\text{incident}} = \lambda_0 (1 - \cos \theta) = \lambda_0 (1 - (-1)) = 2 \lambda_0
\]  
Thus, the maximum change in wavelength is \( 2 \lambda_0 \).  

The given wavelength is \( \lambda = 2.00 \, \text{Å} = 2.00 \times 10^{-10} \, \text{m} \). The Compton wavelength \( \lambda_0 \) is approximately \( 2.43 \times 10^{-12} \, \text{m} \).  
The maximum change in wavelength is:  
\[
\Delta \lambda_{\text{max}} = 2 \lambda_0 = 2 \times 2.43 \times 10^{-12} \, \text{m} = 4.86 \times 10^{-12} \, \text{m}
\]  
The maximum percentage change in wavelength is:  
\[
\text{Percentage change} = \frac{\Delta \lambda}{\lambda} \times 100 = \frac{4.86 \times 10^{-12}}{2.00 \times 10^{-10}} \times 100 = 0.0243 \times 100 = 2.43\%
\]  

Thus, the maximum percentage change in the wavelength is \(\boxed{2.43\%}\).",fluency,"# Task Description
