(a) What is the magnitude of the acceleration of the proton?  
(b) How long does it take the proton to reach a speed of \(35,000 \, \text{m/s}\)?
</USER_REQUEST>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
(a) The force on the proton due to the electric field is \(F = qE\), where \(q\) is the charge and \(E\) is the electric field strength. The force is \(F = (1.60 \times 10^{-19} \, \text{C})(500 \, \text{N/C}) = 8.0 \times 10^{-17} \, \text{N}\).

Using Newton's second law, \(F = ma\), we can find the acceleration:
\[
a = \frac{F}{m} = \frac{8.0 \times 10^{-17} \, \text{N}}{1.67 \times 10^{-27} \, \text{kg}} = 4.79 \times 10^{10} \, \text{m/s}^2
\]

(b) The proton starts from rest, so its initial velocity is \(v_0 = 0\). We use the equation \(v = v_0 + at\), where \(v\) is the final velocity, \(v_0\) is the initial velocity, \(a\) is the acceleration, and \(t\) is the time. We solve for \(t\):
\[
t = \frac{v - v_0}{a} = \frac{35,000 \, \text{m/s} - 0 \, \text{m/s}}{4.79 \times 10^{10} \, \text{m/s}^2} = \frac{3.5 \times 10^4}{4.79 \times 10^{10}} \, \text{s} = 7.32 \times 10^{-7} \, \text{s}
\]

Thus, the time is \(\boxed{7.32 \times 10^{-7} \, \text{s}}\).
