   - Stars are in hydrostatic equilibrium: The inward force of gravity is balanced by the outward pressure generated by nuclear fusion in the core.  
   - As a star evolves, it moves through different stages of fusion, producing heavier elements in its core.  
   - When the star can still fuse hydrogen into helium, it does so in a shell around the core.  
   - When the core's helium is depleted, it can still fuse helium into carbon and oxygen.  
   - For massive stars, this process can continue, producing neon, silicon, and other heavier elements, all the end result of fusion is an iron core.

