The correct answer is $\boxed{\text{Gas particles attract themively}}$. This is because the kinetic molecular theory of gases assumes that there are no intermolecular forces between gas particles. $\boxed{\text{The kinetic molecular theory of gases has the following postulates:}}$ 1. Gases are made up of particles that are in constant, random motion. 2. The volume of the particles is negligible compared to the volume of the gas. 3. The average kinetic energy of the particles is directly proportional to the temperature of the gas. 4. There are no intermolecular forces between gas particles. 5. Collisions between gas particles are elastic, meaning that no energy is lost during the collisions. $\boxed{\text{The statement ""Gas particles attract evenly"" is not a postulate of the kinetic molecular theory of gases.}}$ $\boxed{\text{In fact, the kinetic molecular theory assumes that there are no intermolecular forces between gas particles.}}$
