\[
\text{Work} = \text{Force} \times \text{Disstance} \times \cos(\theta)
\]

where:
- Work is the energy transferred by the force
- Force is the force applied
- Distance is the distance over which the force is applied
- \(\theta\) is the angle between the force vector and the direction of displacement.

Given:
- Force (\(F\)) = 60.0 N
- Angle (\(\theta\)) = 60.0°
- Distance (\(d\)) = 10.0 m

Substituting the values into the formula:

\[
\text{Work} = 60.0 \, \text{N} \times 10.0 \, \text{m} \times \cos(60.0^\circ)
\]

Since \(\cos(60.0^\circ) = 0.5\):

\[
\text{Work} = 600 \, \text{N m} \times 0.5 = 300 \, \text{J}
\]

Thus, the work done is \(\boxed{300 \, \text{J}}\).
</ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>

